---
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const stamps = [
  [
    "One subject",
    "Pass",
    "One object: the slot overlay on the Hangar's ship drawing, gaining a state for a part outside the scored rules. The two Playground conditions, selected and unselected, focus and the linked mount are facets shown by the same built example. Five adjacent items were named.",
  ],
  [
    "Grounded",
    "Fail",
    "Every held-fixed item exists in code or a spec and every open item can be built and judged by looking. One claim was false and one was false in half its cases (findings 1 and 2). Both are wording, not a fault in the question.",
  ],
  [
    "Consequence",
    "Pass",
    "From the section alone: “After this, a slot on the Hangar's ship drawing shows when the part in it is outside the scored rules, so a ship that cannot launch no longer looks ready and the player can see which part has to come off.”",
  ],
] as const;

const findings = [
  [
    "“Up to eight slots share one hull.”",
    "False. The Fiddler has nine mounts: two engine pairs and five hull mounts. Eight is the Corvette. The most crowded ship is the one the answer has to be judged on.",
    "Corrected: nine, and the Fiddler is named as the ship the answer is judged on.",
  ],
  [
    "“A ship that is itself outside the scored rules: its tab carries that.”",
    "False with Playground on: a tab is marked unavailable only with Playground off, and otherwise shows nothing about the class.",
    "The claim is removed. A ship outside the rules stays another question.",
  ],
  [
    "Adjacent: “the launch row's own explanation of why launch is unavailable.”",
    "Does not exist. With Playground off and a blocked ship the Hangar's launch buttons are only unavailable; the explanation is on the title screen. The Playground contract places a status beside the launch actions, and the blocked case is not built.",
    "The section now says the buttons are unavailable without saying why.",
  ],
  [
    "“A mount stays selectable whatever it holds.”",
    "Imprecise. A linked mount is never selectable or focusable; it is reached through its primary and never draws selection corners.",
    "Corrected in the fixed items.",
  ],
  [
    "“Information is never lime.”",
    "Omission. A filled slot already draws its type text in lime, and the design system lists occupied slots under the selected colour.",
    "The section now names the type text as part of what lime already says.",
  ],
  [
    "The list of means the slot has: fill, border, corners, type text, number, a mark of its own.",
    "Omission. Selecting an engine also lights a lime glow on the engine's drawing, the only selection signal a linked mount gets, and Plan draws the hull at 70% opacity.",
    "The shortened section lists no means. Both are carried into exploration as existing means.",
  ],
  [
    "Reach: “the Hangar's Plan view and the Lab's mount tools.”",
    "Incomplete. The Lab's mount planner, the Fiddler pincer editor and two study components (the ion cannon fitting and the Fiddler's flight-ready view) also draw the shared overlay. The Wasp side-slot positioner keeps its own copy.",
    "The section now says wherever it is drawn. The list belongs to scope.",
  ],
  [
    "“The slot language's description of what a filled slot means changes with it.”",
    "Imprecise. No spec describes the slot language beyond one clause in the design system and one in the Hangar contract; it lives in the earlier study page and the drawing code.",
    "Removed. Which documents change belongs to scope.",
  ],
  [
    "“At the Hangar's drawing size.”",
    "Imprecise. The drawing scales to its panel and the ship's bounds, so the nine-slot Fiddler draws its slots smallest.",
    "Replaced by judging on the whole Fiddler.",
  ],
] as const;
---

<StudyLayout>
  <section>
    <h1>Ship slots — question review of 01</h1>
    <p>
      <a href="/lab/studies/ship-slots-2026-11-11">Return to the study</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The first draft of section 01 was tested on 11 November 2026 against the
      part 01 question rubric (<code
        >docs/plans/2026-11-10-part-01-question-rubric.md</code
      >), a proposal and not yet a standing rule. The run used a clean-context
      agent with read-only access, no part in the framing and no expected
      result. This is separate from feasibility, implementation authorization
      and human judgment.
    </p>
    <p>
      One run. The draft it tested carried Fixed, Open, Reach and Adjacent lists
      and ran to about 560 words. After the run the section was cut to about a
      third of that and corrected for every finding below. The shortened section
      has not been tested again; the draft is in the session's working history
      only and was never committed.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Metric</th>
            <th>Stamp</th>
            <th>Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {stamps.map(([metric, stamp, reason]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{metric}</th>
              <td>{stamp}</td>
              <td>{reason}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h2>Findings, most serious first</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>What the draft said</th>
            <th>What the repository shows</th>
            <th>After the run</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {findings.map(([claim, found, after], index) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{index + 1}</th>
              <td>{claim}</td>
              <td>{found}</td>
              <td>{after}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h2>What checked out</h2>
    <p>
      The slot's geometry, markings and states, and that the overlay never reads
      a part's class; slots in Plan only; mounts and what they accept; one class
      for every part in the catalog; the Playground rules, off and on; that the
      class shows only on part rows; the two jobs of lime; the mount's focus
      ring; the two linked studies; and that parts exist to build every open
      case, an engine outside the rules on a mirrored pair included.
    </p>
    <h2>Not checked</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing was rendered. Also unchecked: whether the slot language was
      settled before parts had classes, the title screen's blocked status in
      code, and whether the overlay can read the Playground setting, which is a
      readiness question.
    </p>
    <h2>Human judgment</h2>
    <p>
      On 11 November 2026, while the run was under way, the designer held that
      the draft copied the length of the actions study's opening without that
      study's reach, and set a target of about a third of that length, filled
      only as far as the question needs. The designer also corrected a claim the
      run had passed: the draft said each part's drawing sits inside its slot. A
      slot marks where a part is placed and the part can reach beyond it; Plan
      view scales weapons and utilities down to fit, which is a defect. The
      section now says so.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
.table-wrap {
  overflow-x: auto;
  margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
}
table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
th,
td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
</style>
